<p>After a hailstorm, the first estimate from your insurance company can look official and complete. It has line items, quantities, and a total. But many first estimates are written quickly, from a limited inspection, during the busiest weeks of the year. <strong>Hail damage claim supplements</strong> exist to fix what that first pass misses.</p>

<p>This guide explains what a supplement is, why first scopes come up short in Dallas, how Xactimate estimates are built, the items carriers commonly leave out, and how to submit a supplement that an adjuster can actually approve. It reflects the way a Dallas Public Adjuster reviews hail estimates every season, but every step here is something a homeowner can do.</p>

<h2>What is a hail damage claim supplement?</h2>

<p>A supplement is a request to revise the insurer’s estimate on an existing claim. It usually covers one or more of these:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Missed items:</strong> damage or components the adjuster did not include</li>
<li><strong>Quantity corrections:</strong> roof area, ridge length, or gutter footage that was under-measured</li>
<li><strong>Pricing or labor corrections:</strong> the wrong material grade, or missing steep-roof or high-roof charges</li>
<li><strong>Hidden damage:</strong> conditions found during repairs, such as broken or soft decking</li>
<li><strong>Code items:</strong> work required by the building code to complete the repair, where the policy covers it</li>
</ul>

<p>A supplement stays under the same claim number. It is not a new claim, and it is not a complaint. It simply asks the insurer to update its numbers based on better information.</p>

<h2>Why first estimates come up short in Dallas</h2>

<p>Dallas County sees a lot of hail. NOAA’s Storm Events Database lists 841 hail reports in the county since 1955, and the busiest years have topped 50 reports. When a large storm hits, thousands of claims can arrive in days. On <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=306259" target="_blank" rel="noopener">May 24, 2011</a>, for example, NOAA logged grapefruit-size hail (4.25 inches) in Irving that broke windows, while the same storm system brought <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=306783" target="_blank" rel="noopener">baseball-size hail</a> to Skillman Street and Southwestern Boulevard in Dallas. On <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=766971" target="_blank" rel="noopener">June 6, 2018</a>, 3-inch hail fell near the President George Bush Turnpike in Carrollton and in <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=766939" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coppell</a>.</p>

<p>Storms like these create predictable pressure points:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Volume.</strong> Adjusters may carry large caseloads and have limited time on each roof.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection method.</strong> Some inspections rely on drones, satellite measurements, or a ground-level look, which can miss soft-metal damage and roof accessories.</li>
<li><strong>Focus on the obvious.</strong> Broken shingles get written; bruised mats, cracked seal strips, and dented flashing are easier to overlook.</li>
<li><strong>Pricing lag.</strong> Estimating software uses regional price lists. After a big storm, local labor and material costs can move faster than a price list.</li>
<li><strong>Hidden conditions.</strong> Decking and underlayment damage often cannot be seen until the old roof comes off.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How Xactimate estimates are built</h2>

<p>Most property insurers write estimates in Xactimate. Each line item represents a task and material, such as “remove laminated composition shingles” or “drip edge,” with a unit (square, linear foot, each) and a regional price. The total depends on two things: which line items are included, and how much of each.</p>

<p>That structure is useful. If you or your contractor prepare a line-item estimate in the same format, the comparison becomes simple. Instead of arguing that the total is “too low,” you can point to the specific items and quantities that differ. Adjusters respond far better to “line 14 measures 26 squares; our measurement is 31, see attached report” than to a general complaint.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Claim-Supplements-What-Carriers-Miss-in-Their-Scope-2-1024x683.webp" alt="Two men inspecting hail damage on a shingle roof" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A second, slope-by-slope roof inspection is often where missed components and under-measured areas come to light.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Commonly missed line items on hail claims</h2>

<p>These are items that frequently need to be added or corrected on Dallas-area hail estimates. Whether each one is owed depends on your policy and the actual condition of the property.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Why it gets missed</th><th>How to support it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Roof measurements and waste</td><td>Aerial or quick measurements; waste factor too low for hips and valleys</td><td>Measurement report, slope diagram, explanation of roof complexity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Starter course and hip/ridge cap</td><td>Treated as included in field shingles</td><td>Manufacturer installation instructions, linear-foot measurements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drip edge</td><td>Not visible from the ground; sometimes absent on the old roof</td><td>Photos of the eaves and rakes; code reference if required</td></tr>
<tr><td>Step and counter flashing</td><td>Assumed reusable</td><td>Photos of dents, bends, or damage during removal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pipe jacks, turtle vents, exhaust caps</td><td>Easy to overlook on a crowded roof</td><td>Close-up photos with chalk marks and a count</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional shingle layers</td><td>Inspector did not check the edge or a vent opening</td><td>Photo at the eave or a cut-out showing two layers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steep and high-roof labor</td><td>Pitch or story height not recorded</td><td>Pitch gauge photo, two-story elevation photo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decking replacement</td><td>Cannot be seen until tear-off</td><td>Photos during tear-off, sheet count, notice to the insurer before covering</td></tr>
<tr><td>Permits and code items</td><td>Left for the contractor to absorb</td><td>Permit record, code requirement, ordinance or law coverage on the policy</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>How small gaps add up</h2>

<p>Individually, most missed items look minor. Together they can change the outcome of a claim. Consider a hypothetical two-story Dallas home with a hip roof:</p>
<ul>
<li>The roof is measured a few squares short, and the waste factor is set for a simple gable roof rather than a hip roof with valleys.</li>
<li>Starter and drip edge are left off because they were not visible in the photos.</li>
<li>Steep-roof and two-story labor charges are missing because pitch and height were not recorded.</li>
<li>Gutters were included, but not the detach-and-reset needed to install new drip edge behind them.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of these is a dramatic error, and none requires proving new damage. Each simply needs a measurement or photo. That is why careful line-by-line review is often more productive than arguing about the total. It can also matter for whether the claim exceeds the deductible at all: a scope that is short on several small items may come in below a percentage wind/hail deductible, while a complete scope may not.</p>

<h2>Beyond the roof: exterior, interior, and mechanical items</h2>

<p>Hail rarely stops at the shingles. Items that often go missing from the scope include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gutters and downspouts.</strong> Dents and crushed sections, plus detach-and-reset of gutters during roof work.</li>
<li><strong>Siding, trim, and paint.</strong> Cracked or dented siding, chipped trim, and the painting needed to match repaired areas.</li>
<li><strong>Windows and screens.</strong> Torn screens and damaged window wraps or frames.</li>
<li><strong>The AC condenser.</strong> Bent coil fins can affect performance; a qualified HVAC technician’s report helps.</li>
<li><strong>Fences, pergolas, and outdoor fixtures.</strong> Coverage for “other structures” is usually listed separately on the declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Interior damage.</strong> Ceiling stains, wet insulation, and drywall repairs from roof leaks.</li>
<li><strong>General conditions.</strong> Debris removal, dumpster, and protection of landscaping or the driveway.</li>
</ul>

<p>Commercial buildings, apartments, and warehouses with flat or low-slope TPO or modified-bitumen roofs raise their own scope questions: membrane punctures that only show up under close inspection or testing, damaged rooftop HVAC units and curbs, skylights, drains, and the insulation beneath a wet membrane. Those claims usually need a more detailed inspection than a quick walk of the surface. Our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page covers them in more detail.</p>

<p>Brick-veneer homes, which are common across Dallas County, can hide hail damage in places like soffits, fascia, and window wraps rather than the brick itself. Check those carefully.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Building codes, permits, and supplements</h2>

<p>Re-roofing generally requires a permit from the City of Dallas Planning &amp; Development Department, and nearby cities such as Garland, Irving, Mesquite, and Richardson run their own permit and inspection offices. Repairs must meet the city’s adopted International Residential Code and International Building Code, which can require items the old roof never had.</p>

<p>That does not mean the insurer automatically pays for every code item. Payment for code-required work depends on your ordinance or law coverage and its limit. When a code item is needed, document the requirement (a permit note, inspector comment, or code section from your contractor) and submit it as part of the supplement. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page explains how this works in Dallas.</p>

<h2>Step by step: how to submit a hail damage supplement</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Get the full insurer estimate.</strong> Ask for the complete line-item estimate, not just the summary page, and the photos and measurements behind it.</li>
<li><strong>Compare it line by line.</strong> Put the insurer’s estimate next to your contractor’s scope. Mark every missing item and every quantity that differs.</li>
<li><strong>Document each difference.</strong> One photo, measurement, or document for every item you want added.</li>
<li><strong>Write a short cover letter.</strong> List the items, the reason for each, and the attachments. Keep it factual.</li>
<li><strong>Report hidden damage immediately.</strong> If decking or other hidden damage is found during tear-off, stop, photograph it, and notify the insurer so it can inspect before the area is covered.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up in writing.</strong> Note the date you sent the supplement. Texas’s Prompt Payment of Claims law generally requires insurers to decide within 15 business days after receiving all requested items, or notify you that more time is needed.</li>
</ol>

<p>For photo standards, see <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</a>.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Claim-Supplements-What-Carriers-Miss-in-Their-Scope-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Contractor holding a tablet and talking with homeowners in front of their house" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Reviewing the contractor’s scope against the insurer’s estimate helps identify exactly which items to supplement.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How payment works on a supplemented claim</h2>

<p>On a replacement cost policy, the insurer typically pays the actual cash value of approved items first and holds back recoverable depreciation until the work is done. Approved supplement items usually follow the same pattern. Your wind/hail deductible is generally applied once to the claim, not again for each supplement. Watch your policy’s deadline for completing repairs and submitting final invoices to recover depreciation. Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> shows how these pieces fit together.</p>

<h2>What weakens a supplement</h2>
<ul>
<li>Asking for items with no photo or measurement behind them</li>
<li>Sending a contractor’s lump-sum bid with no line items</li>
<li>Covering hidden damage before the insurer has been notified</li>
<li>Waiting months after the work is finished to submit</li>
<li>Including items that are clearly wear, maintenance, or upgrades beyond like kind and quality</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Claim-Supplements-What-Carriers-Miss-in-Their-Scope-3-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster talking with a couple outside a brick home with a blue tarp on the roof" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Clear communication about what was missed, and why, makes a supplement easier for the carrier to review.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>If the supplement is denied</h2>

<p>Ask for the reason in writing, item by item. Some denials can be resolved with one more photo or a code citation. Others reflect a true disagreement about the scope or price. If the disagreement is over the amount of a covered loss, your policy’s appraisal clause may apply; see our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page. If the insurer is unresponsive, you can contact the Texas Department of Insurance. For questions about lawsuits or deadlines, talk to a Texas attorney. Our guide on <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-hail-claim-was-denied-in-texas/">what to do if your hail claim was denied</a> covers these steps in more depth.</p>

<h2>When to get help with a supplement</h2>

<p>If the gap between your contractor’s scope and the insurer’s estimate is large, or you have gone back and forth more than once, a public adjuster may help. A Dallas Public Adjuster can review the policy, re-inspect the property, write a line-item Xactimate estimate, and present the supplement to the carrier on your behalf. No one can guarantee the outcome. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

